course: dictionary
lesson: 14412
title: Overfill: too much in the container
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To overfill something is to put too much into it, so it becomes too full and spills over. The prefix over- means too much, as in overcook or overflow. «Do not overfill the kettle, or the water will spill out.» The word tells you the kettle has more water in it than it should hold.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. The words «spill out» tell you what happens when you overfill. Ask yourself: what would happen if I poured more in? If the answer is that it spills, then overfill means too much.
explain: In maths, a container overfills when you pour in more than its capacity. Capacity is how much a container can hold. «A jug holds 1 litre, so 1.5 litres overfills it.» The extra half litre has nowhere to go, so it spills.
fact: Overfill means to put too much into something, so it spills over.
fact: The prefix over- means too much, as in overcook or overflow.
fact: «Overfill» is a verb: you overfill, it overfills, it overfilled, it is overfilling.
fact: Capacity is the most a container can hold.
fact: Safety rule: never overfill a kettle, a bath or a fuel tank.
exercise: «Do not overfill the kettle, or the water will spill out.» What does overfill mean? | Put the kettle away in a cupboard | Boil the water very quickly | Clean the kettle with a cloth | *Put too much in, so it spills over
exercise: A jug holds 1 litre. Mia pours in 1.5 litres. What happens? | The jug grows to hold 1.5 litres | The jug stays empty | *The extra half litre spills over
exercise: «The tank overfilled, and petrol ran down the side.» Find and copy one word that shows the tank had too much in it. | petrol | side | tank | *overfilled
test: Ben filled the cup to the top, and Mum said, «Don't overfill it!» What does «overfill the cup» mean? | Wash the cup carefully | *Pour in so much that it spills over | Drink all of it very quickly
test: Which is a safety rule about overfilling? | *Never overfill a kettle, bath or fuel tank | Overfill the bath to make it deeper | Pour as much as you can into a fuel tank
exam: Mia says overfill means the same as fill. Is she right? | Yes, they mean exactly the same | *No, overfill means too much, so it spills over | No, overfill means completely empty | Yes, but only for cups
